@meta
id: 21-16
title: Drill — paste, fix, yank back
chapter: 21
type: drill
skills: macro-edit
par: 32
minutes: 4
reps: 6
vscode: no equivalent
open: status-codes.ts
vimrc: let @a="^f,s: \<Esc>A;\<Esc>j"

@brief
Register `a` holds a macro that turns `ORDER_NOT_FOUND,404` into
`ORDER_NOT_FOUND: 404;`. The semicolon should be a comma. Paste the
macro, fix the one character, yank it back, run it on all five lines.
Six times, until the round trip is a reflex.

@keys
| `"ap` | macro into the file |
| `0"ay$` | line back into the macro |
| `r,` | replace the character under the cursor with a comma |

@hints
`Go<Esc>"ap` puts it on a fresh last line. `0f;r,` fixes it. `0"ay$`
takes it back, `dd` cleans up.
--
After `"ap` the cursor is at the end of the pasted text, so start the
fix with `0`. `f;` from the end of the line finds nothing.

@solution
    G o <Esc> "ap   0f; r,   0"ay$   dd   ggj   5@a   :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes. Paste, fix, `0"ay$`, `dd`. The `0` before the
yank and the `$` at its end are the two things worth drilling: one
makes sure you take the whole macro, the other makes sure you do not
take the newline.

@start status-codes.ts
export const statusCodes = {
  ORDER_NOT_FOUND,404
  ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED,409
  CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND,404
  PAYMENT_DECLINED,402
  REFUND_FAILED,502
};

@expected status-codes.ts
export const statusCodes = {
  ORDER_NOT_FOUND: 404,
  ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED: 409,
  CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND: 404,
  PAYMENT_DECLINED: 402,
  REFUND_FAILED: 502,
};
